when a small group of living organisms (small population) is separated from the main population from which they originated (for example, if they pass through the mountain range or river, or if they move to a new island from which they cannot easily come back), then they often find themselves in an environment that is different from the one in which they were previously.
This new environment has other resources and other competitors, so the new population will need other characteristics or changes, compared to those that it had before to become a strong rival.
The initial population hasn’t changed at all – it still needs the same adaptations as before.
Over the course of a time, as the new population begins to adapt to a new environment, it begins to look less and less like the rest of the population.
Over time, in a thousand or even millions of years, these two populations will look so different that it cannot be said that it is the same species.
We call this process the formation of species, that is, the emergence of new species. The formation of species is an obligatory consequence and an extremely significant part of evolution.
