Technological determinism is a term that covers a wide range of practical ideas ranging from technical progress or a technological impermanifience to a strict point of view that the destinies of people is driven by a fundamental logic associated with the laws of science and their manifestation in technology.
Most views on technological determinism share two general ideas: that the direction for the development of technologies is almost uncontrollable to cultural or political influence and that, in its in turn, the “impactation” of technologies on society is conditioned by their own properties, and not by society.
For example, it would be possible to say that the car is the mandatory reason for the development of the roads.
However, the national road network is economically unprofitable only for a small group of cars, so new methods of production are being developed, allowing to reduce the cost of owning a car.
Mass possession of cars also leads to an increase in the number of accidents on the roads, which leads to the invention of new methods of treatment of received injuries.
